      "site_admin": false
    },
    "created_at": "2025-01-17T01:46:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-01-17T01:46:38Z",
    "body": "Even if the implementation makes the implementation defined choices in [try to scroll to the fragment](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsing-the-web.html#try-to-scroll-to-the-fragment) of:\n\n- Not waiting at all (ignoring the stated performance reasons)\n- Never having any reason to believe that the user is no longer interested in scrolling to the fragment\n\nScrolling to the fragment may _never_ happen as 'try to scroll the fragment' has the normative requirement of \"its parser has [stopped parsing](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#stop-parsing),\". If I understand correctly, stopping parsing happens before either the load event or DOMContentLoaded events have fired. Not scrolling so early on also doesn't seem like the intent to me.\n\nIt's a bit outside of the scope of fixing the WPT test in OP, but I think that's what Anne is referring to above - I probably would need to do some more testing here of how browsers behave.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
